   "subject": "Deterministic assignment of data to stochastic nodes?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I am trying to create a model with nodes for which values should be calculated deterministically from other nodes, but the probability of the nodes depends on a distribution. For a minimal example, imagine we are attempting to infer the intensity lambda of a Poisson process using data from a sampler with detection probability <i>p</i>. I&#39;ve unsuccessfully tried:<div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nimble</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>modelCode </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nimbleCode</span><span style=\"color:#660\">({</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">lambda</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    y_detected</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> p<br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>  r </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dgamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  p </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dbeta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">})</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">set</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">seed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">42</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>n </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>samples </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> round</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">lambda</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y_detected </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> samples</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>constants </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>inits </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">r </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">7</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> p </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>m </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nimbleModel</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">modelCode</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                 constants </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> constants</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                 data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                 inits </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> inits</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                 check </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><div><br>The above code results in the data I intended to supply for y_detected being overwritten with NA. I&#39;ve also tried:</div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">modelCode </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nimbleCode</span><span style=\"color:#660\">({</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">lambda</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> round</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y_detected</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> p</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>  r </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dgamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  p </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dbeta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">})</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><div><br>But the above results in multiple definitions of `y[i]`.</div><div><br></div><div>The complication is obviously that the value has to be updated as stochastic dependencies are sampled. What&#39;s the best way to code something like this?</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Gregory</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ywBrcOwZAgAJ",
   "from": "Gregory Penn",
   "author": "4a248770402c04bc",
   "date": "2019-11-03T02:01:56Z",
   "subject": "Re: Deterministic assignment of data to stochastic nodes?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I may have figured this out. It seems that while</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">lambda</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    y_detected</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> p<br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>inside the `nimbleCode` overwrites any data set as argument to `nimbleModel`, the solution is just to set the data after building the model with <div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">m$setData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><br>I believe that the dependency graph is correct, </div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/nimble-users/attach/219ec706b00cb/testgraph.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"testgraph.png\" width=\"320\" height=\"290\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\"></p><div>And running the model with a sufficiently large sample and iterations of the MCMC produces reasonable posterior distributions. There is something I&#39;m still not understanding, though.  <div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">m$isDataVars</span></div></code></div>Returns a list with an element named for each variable: y, y_detected, p, and r. The values for y and y_detected reflect the number of nodes with those names. The values for p and r appear to be empty, however. They appear as `numeric(0)`. I don&#39;t understand the empty values, and I don&#39;t understand why m$isData(&quot;y&quot;) returns a vector of FALSE, while m$isData seems to suggest that it is being treated as data. Can someone please clarify? Or are there help files for these functions that I haven&#39;t found? Also, is it possible to inspect the likelihood function for a given node?</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Gregory</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "xyvM4QfaEgAJ",
   "from": "Chris Paciorek",
   "author": "5fa2a363d4ddd1d1",
   "date": "2019-11-05T15:54:57Z",
   "subject": "Re: Deterministic assignment of data to stochastic nodes?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">hi Gregory,<div><br></div><div>In NIMBLE, the notion of &#39;data&#39; is for stochastic nodes for which one knows the values. I.e., for terms that contribute to the likelihood.<br></div><div><br></div><div>However, in your code:</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>    y[i] ~ dpois(lambda = r)<br>    y_detected[i] &lt;- y[i] * p<br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>&#39;y_detected&#39; here is deterministic and so it would not contribute to the likelihood.</div><div><br></div><div>On top of that, I would think that y_detected is integer-valued, but y[i]*p will be a real-valued number based on your code. </div><div><br></div><div>I don&#39;t know for sure what you intended / what makes sense for your context, but I would think that y_detected would be from a binomial distribution, like this:</div><div><br></div><div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>   y[i] ~ dpois(lambda = r)<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>   y_detected[i] ~ dbin(p, y[i])<br></div><div><br></div><div>Then y[i] is latent and would be sampled as part of an MCMC, and you would set &#39;y_detected&#39; to be data.</div><div><br></div><div>-Chris</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Sat, Nov 2, 2019 at 7:01 PM Gregory Penn &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">gregor...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I may have figured this out. It seems that while<br><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">for</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">i </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">in</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">:</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">3</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">{</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>    y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">i</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">~</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">lambda</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> r</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>    y_detected</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">i</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">i</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> p<br>  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">}</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>inside the `nimbleCode` overwrites any data set as argument to `nimbleModel`, the solution is just to set the data after building the model with <div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">m$setData</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">data</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span></div></code></div><br>I believe that the dependency graph is correct, </div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/nimble-users/attach/12da07e1cc2bc7/testgraph.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"testgraph.png\" width=\"320\" height=\"290\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\"></p><div>And running the model with a sufficiently large sample and iterations of the MCMC produces reasonable posterior distributions. There is something I&#39;m still not understanding, though.  <div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">m$isDataVars</span></div></code></div>Returns a list with an element named for each variable: y, y_detected, p, and r. The values for y and y_detected reflect the number of nodes with those names. The values for p and r appear to be empty, however. They appear as `numeric(0)`. I don&#39;t understand the empty values, and I don&#39;t understand why m$isData(&quot;y&quot;) returns a vector of FALSE, while m$isData seems to suggest that it is being treated as data. Can someone please clarify? Or are there help files for these functions that I haven&#39;t found? Also, is it possible to inspect the likelihood function for a given node?</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Gregory</div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;nimble-users&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nimble-users...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo view this discussion on the web visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/nimble-users/7ca73f14-cd87-47da-acd0-9e477509f718%40googlegroups.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/nimble-users/7ca73f14-cd87-47da-acd0-9e477509f718%2540googlegroups.com?utm_medium%3Demail%26utm_source%3Dfooter&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792478303734000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1ye7rdTBa8Y9fHbAyW3APa\">https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/nimble-users/7ca73f14-cd87-47da-acd0-9e477509f718%40googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\n</blockquote></div>\r\n"
